# The Marathas and the 18th Century Struggle for Power

> In the eighteenth century the Marathas rose from hill-fort guerrillas to
> the dominant power of the subcontinent — Shivaji's Swarajya, the Peshwas'
> confederacy, and the disaster of Panipat 1761 — while successor states,
> Anglo-French wars, and a trading company from London fought over what
> the Mughals had lost.

## 1. Background: The Maratha Country

The western Deccan's mountains, the collapse of Ahmadnagar and Bijapur, and
**Malik Ambar's** model of guerrilla war and cash-settled revenue prepared
the ground. The **Bhosle** family of the Deccani sultanates' service
produced **Shivaji** (1630-1680), son of **Shahji Bhosle** and the
formidable **Jijabai**, born at **Shivneri** fort and trained in the Poona
jagir under his guardian Dadaji Konddev.

## 2. Shivaji (1630-1680)

| Year | Event |
| --- | --- |
| 1646 | Captures Torna, the first fort |
| 1659 | Kills Bijapur's Afzal Khan at Pratapgad (tiger claws) |
| 1663 | Night raid wounds Mughal viceroy Shaista Khan in Poona |
| 1664, 1670 | Sacks Surat, the Mughal west-coast port |
| 1665 | Treaty of Purandar with Jai Singh I: cedes 23 forts |
| 1666 | Escapes house arrest in Agra |
| 1674 | Coronation as Chhatrapati at Raigad, 6 June |
| 1680 | Dies at Raigad, aged 50 |

The coronation by the Varanasi Brahmin **Gaga Bhatt** gave the upstart
king ritual legitimacy — and the title **Haindava Dharmodharak**. With
roughly 300 forts, an infantry of Mavali hillmen, and light cavalry,
Shivaji built a state, **Swarajya**, on mobility and cash salaries rather
than hereditary jagirs.

## 3. Shivaji's Statecraft

- **Ashtapradhan:** the eight-minister council — Peshwa (prime minister),
  Amatya (finance), Mantri (chronicler), Sachiv (correspondence), Senapati
  (commander), Sumant (foreign affairs), Nyayadhish (justice), and Panditrao
  (religion). Advisory, appointed, non-hereditary — until the Peshwas made
  their post otherwise.
- **Chauth:** one-fourth of a territory's revenue, paid in exchange for
  not being raided.
- **Sardeshmukhi:** an additional one-tenth claimed as a hereditary
  overlord's due.
- Revenue settled directly with the cultivator in the manner of Malik
  Ambar's system; village officers — patil, kulkarni, deshmukh — kept the
  accounts.

[definition]
**Chauth and sardeshmukhi** were Maratha claims on neighbouring lands:
together they could take 35% of a region's revenue without annexing it —
protection economics that made the Marathas a standing tax on the Mughal
successor states.
[/definition]

## 4. The Peshwas

After **Sambhaji's** execution (1689), **Rajaram's** exile, and Tarabai's
regency, **Shahu** — released by the Mughals in 1707 — won the civil war
and moved the real government to his Peshwas.

- **Balaji Vishwanath (1713-1720):** marched to Delhi in 1719 to back the
  Sayyid brothers, and returned with sanads granting **chauth and
  sardeshmukhi over the six Mughal provinces of the Deccan** — legalising
  the Maratha claim and making the Peshwaship hereditary.
- **Baji Rao I (1720-1740):** the greatest Peshwa and perhaps India's
  finest eighteenth-century general. He crushed the Nizam at **Palkhed
  (1728)** and **Bhopal (1737)**, raided Delhi's gates, and opened Malwa,
  Bundelkhand (Chhatrasal paid with a third of his lands), and Gujarat to
  Maratha collectors. He died at thirty-nine, undefeated in battle.
- **Balaji Baji Rao (Nana Saheb, 1740-1761):** consolidated, pushed into
  Punjab, and inherited the gamble that ended at Panipat.

## 5. The Third Battle of Panipat, 14 January 1761

The Marathas' northern march — reaching **Attock in 1758** — provoked
**Ahmad Shah Abdali's** final invasion. With the Rohilla **Najib-ud-Daula**
and Awadh's **Shuja-ud-Daula** beside him, Abdali faced **Sadashivrao
Bhau's** army.

| Feature | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Maratha army | 45,000+ cavalry, Ibrahim Khan Gardi's French-trained infantry |
| Ally who left | Suraj Mal the Jat, over conduct and claims |
| Casualties | The Peshwa's heir Vishwasrao, Bhau himself, and most of the army |
| Aftermath | Abdali returned home without gains; Punjab passed to the Sikhs |

[warning]
Panipat did not destroy the Marathas — within a decade they were back in
the north under Mahadji Scindia — but it drained their best leadership,
and in the vacuum the English East India Company, already masters of
Bengal since Plassey-Buxar, expanded unchecked.
[/warning]

## 6. The Confederacy

@diagram tree id="maratha-confederacy" layout="tree" aria="The Maratha confederacy's five great houses and their seats"
  Maratha Confederacy -> "Peshwa (Poona)"
  Maratha Confederacy -> "Gaekwad (Baroda)"
  Maratha Confederacy -> "Scindia (Gwalior)"
  Maratha Confederacy -> "Holkar (Indore)"
  Maratha Confederacy -> "Bhonsle (Nagpur)"
@/diagram

Each house ran its own army and foreign policy: **Mahadji Scindia**
restored Shah Alam II to Delhi (1772) and modernised his battalions under
De Boigne; **Ahilyabai Holkar** of Indore (1767-1795) governed so well that
she remains a byword for just kingship. At sea, **Kanhoji Angre**, Sarkhel
of Kolaba (1698-1729), made Maratha naval power supreme on the Konkan
coast. Defeat in the Second and Third Anglo-Maratha Wars (1803-05,
1817-18) ended the confederacy; Baji Rao II surrendered his claims in
1818.

## 7. India in the Eighteenth Century

| State | Founder / Key ruler |
| --- | --- |
| Hyderabad | Nizam-ul-Mulk, 1724 |
| Awadh | Saadat Khan; Shuja-ud-Daula at Panipat |
| Bengal | Murshid Quli Khan; Alivardi Khan; Siraj-ud-Daulah |
| Mysore | Haider Ali (1761-82), Tipu Sultan (1782-99) |
| Sikh misls | Ranjit Singh unites them at Lahore, 1799 |
| Jat Bharatpur | Suraj Mal |

The **Carnatic wars** between the French and the English ran through this
world: the first (1746-48) echoed Europe's War of Austrian Succession; the
second (1749-54) made **Dupleix's** proxy warfare and Clive's defence of
**Arcot** famous; the third ended with **Wandiwash (22 January 1760)**,
where Eyre Coote defeated the Comte de Lally — after which France kept
only unfortified trading posts.

## 8. Why the English Won

[summary]
- **Sea power:** the Royal Navy ruled the routes; Pondicherry starved.
- **Finance:** Bengal's revenues after 1757 and the Company's credit
  outlasted every Indian treasury.
- **Army:** disciplined, regularly paid sepoys with modern artillery.
- **Diplomacy:** subsidiary alliances split the states — Marathas,
  Mysore, Nizam, each fought alone.
- **Politics:** no Indian power pursued a common front; courts ploted
  against their own generals.
[/summary]

## Key Takeaways

- Shivaji's Swarajya (coronation 1674) fused hill guerrilla war with an
  eight-minister state and the chauth-sardeshmukhi revenue engine.
- The Peshwas — Balaji Vishwanath's sanads and Baji Rao I's conquests —
  turned it into a pan-Indian power.
- Panipat (1761) broke the Maratha surge, not the state; the
  confederacy's houses dominated politics until 1818.
- The eighteenth century's real winner was the East India Company: sea
  power, Bengal's money, and divided opponents.
